# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Exercises", "Swapping two variables".
id: swap-temp
title: Swapping two variables with a temporary variable
topic: Sequential composition
level: 1
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Exercises, "Swapping two variables"
tags: assignment, sequence, wp, consequence

task:
  The program swaps the values of $x$ and $y$ with a temporary variable
  $t$. $A$ and $B$ are logical variables: the program does not change
  them, and they name the initial values of $x$ and $y$.

  Calculate the weakest precondition of the program for the postcondition
  $Q$, statement by statement from the last one. Then use the consequence
  rule to show that the triple is valid.

pre: x = A && y = B
post: x = B && y = A

program:
  {P}
  {R3}
  t := x;      // i1
  {R2}
  x := y;      // i2
  {R1}
  y := t       // i3
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of y := t
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i3
text:
  The precondition of an assignment is obtained by substituting the
  assigned variable ($y$) with the assignment expression ($t$) in the
  postcondition $Q$.
calc:
  wp(y := t, Q)
  = (x = B && y = A)[y <- t]
  = x = B && t = A        // y is replaced by t; x = B has no y
set: R1 = x = B && t = A

step: R2, the weakest precondition of x := y
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i2
text:
  We substitute the assigned variable ($x$) with the assignment expression
  ($y$) in the postcondition of this statement, which is $R1$.
calc:
  wp(x := y, R1)
  = (x = B && t = A)[x <- y]
  = y = B && t = A        // x is replaced by y; t = A has no x
set: R2 = y = B && t = A

step: R3, the weakest precondition of t := x
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
text:
  We substitute the assigned variable ($t$) with the assignment expression
  ($x$) in the postcondition of this statement, which is $R2$.
calc:
  wp(t := x, R2)
  = (y = B && t = A)[t <- x]
  = y = B && x = A        // t is replaced by x; y = B has no t
set: R3 = y = B && x = A

step: P implies R3
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R3
text:
  $R3$ is the weakest precondition of the whole program for $Q$. By the
  consequence rule, the triple is valid when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R3$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R3»
vc: x = A && y = B ==> y = B && x = A
why:
  $P$ and $R3$ have the same two conjuncts in a different order, and the
  order of the conjuncts of $&&$ does not matter.
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  Going backwards, each assignment renames one variable in the assertion.
  The weakest precondition is $P$ itself with its conjuncts in a different
  order.
